# WebAssembly architecture configuration
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

config WASM
	bool
	select ARCH_IS_SET
	select ATOMIC_OPERATIONS_BUILTIN
	select USE_SWITCH
	select USE_SWITCH_SUPPORTED
	select ARCH_HAS_CUSTOM_BUSY_WAIT
	select IRQ_OFFLOAD_NESTED if IRQ_OFFLOAD
	help
	  WebAssembly architecture. The kernel runs freestanding in a single
	  wasm32 linear memory; the host supplies devices as imported
	  functions rather than as memory-mapped registers.

if WASM

menu "WebAssembly Options"

config ARCH
	string
	default "wasm"

# Context switching runs on Binaryen's Asyncify transform for now. Everything
# goes through one internal interface so a stack-switching backend can replace
# it later without touching the rest of the arch.
config WASM_ASYNCIFY
	bool
	default y
	help
	  Use Asyncify to save and restore thread stacks.

config WASM_ASYNCIFY_BUFFER_SIZE
	int "Bytes of each thread stack reserved for the Asyncify buffer"
	default 4096
	help
	  Every thread stack object is split in two: the low part is the C
	  shadow stack, the high part is the Asyncify buffer that holds
	  unwound wasm frames.

	  Spike C measured the buffer needing roughly 88 bytes plus 32 per
	  frame live when the thread yields. Asyncify does not bounds-check
	  this buffer: overflowing it corrupts whatever follows, silently and
	  without a trap, so the default is deliberately generous.

config WASM_SAFEPOINTS
	bool "Check for pending interrupts at loop back-edges"
	default y
	help
	  Nothing preempts a running wasm function, so without this a thread
	  that never yields cannot be interrupted at all, and time slicing
	  does not work.

	  With it, a post-link pass inserts a call to z_wasm_safepoint at the
	  top of every loop body. The cost is one load and branch per
	  iteration of every loop in the image.

config WASM_SAFEPOINTS_PER_TICK
	int "Safepoints between progress reports to the host"
	default 20000
	depends on WASM_SAFEPOINTS
	help
	  Under virtual time the clock only advances when the kernel idles, so
	  a thread that spins without calling the kernel would freeze it. Every
	  this many safepoints, the guest gives the host a chance to advance
	  time and raise a deadline.

	  Lower is more responsive and slower; higher is the reverse. This is
	  the knob that decides how coarse preemption is for code that never
	  calls into the kernel.

config WASM_RUN_MAX_TIME_MS
	int "Guest time limit for `west build -t run`"
	default 60000
	help
	  Bounds a run started through the build system, which is also how
	  twister drives this board.

config WASM_RUN_REALTIME
	bool "Follow the wall clock for `west build -t run`"
	help
	  Off by default, so runs are reproducible.

config WASM_IRQ_LINES
	int "Number of host interrupt lines"
	default 16
	help
	  Size of the software ISR table. Interrupts arrive cooperatively: the
	  host sets a pending word in linear memory and the kernel notices it
	  at a safepoint.

endmenu

endif # WASM
